<u> 18-SEP-2005 9:23 PM (NZ Time)</u>
M and I woke up to our first cold morning in the van.  I'd estimate it was about 5 degC.  While the van did have a heater, it only ran on electricity and free camping means no electricity.  We decided not to cook breakfast today so we could get a quick move onto Te Anau which would be our goal for today.  Therefore we had cold cereal.  We needed to wash our faces and brush our teeth.  The other thing we were not sure about since we hastily received our van was if there was water in the tanks and if there was, how long had it been in there? Not wanting to take the risk, we drove to a nearby park which had a bathroom with a toilet and running water, though it was ice cold.  We weren't the only ones free camping. We noticed another couple who lived out of their van too and they were occupying one of the bathrooms so I let M go first while I watched over the van.  Eventually, the other bathroom became free and I cleaned up as well.  There's nothing like ice cold water splashing on your face to wake you up quickly.
<p>
Getting ready to leave the park, we noticed the nice view of the mountain called the Remarkables.  However, there really wasn't any snow and this was an area popular for skiing.  We drove back into town where I stopped off at the visitor's center to find out what thing we could do in Te Anau.  The popular thing was to go see the glowworm caves.  We had missed seeing the glowworms in action in Australia, so I thought it might be fun and that M would enjoy it a lot.  I found out the next tour was at 2 PM so we had a few hours to get there and it's about 2 hours to drive.
<p>
The drive to Te Anau was pretty uneventful, but it was a small town with a big lake nearby.  We went to the a tour booking office so we can setup a tour for the Te Anau Glowworm Caves.  Part of the tour would consist of taking a raft inside the cave to see the glowworms.  Since we had some extra time, I decided to also see what I could book for Milford Sound, our destination for tomorrow.  Kayaking immediately caught my eye, so I signed up for it.  I had imagined it in my head, how it would be kayaking amongst the tall snow-capped mountains in peaceful waters.  It was something I was sure I would never forget.
<p>
We still had about about 2 hours to kill, so we decided to go to the town of Manipouri.  I had read in my guidebook that place was like a postcard picture and after all, we were in the Fiordlands National Park.  A short 20 min drive out to Manipouri 
